Object.assign(LANG.en.skills,{
model:{name:"Modelling",about:"Diagrams that align the team before the code: use cases, classes and sequences.",client:"Casa das Letras community library",title:"Three people, three systems in mind",
 story:"Before coding the loans, the team sat down with Mr Antônio, a volunteer and a reader. Each described \"the system\", and they sounded like three different products: for one, a book had several copies; for another, every copy was a book. Nobody was wrong; a shared picture was missing. A simple diagram on the wall settled in twenty minutes what three meetings had not.",
 theory:[["A model is a simplification with a purpose","A diagram shows only what matters to answer one question: who uses what, which data exist, in what order things happen. It aligns people before any code exists."],
  ["Three views that complete each other","Use cases show who does what (functional view). The class diagram shows concepts and their relationships (static view). The sequence diagram shows messages exchanged over time (dynamic view)."],
  ["Relationships and multiplicity","A Book has many Copies; a Copy belongs to a single Book. Writing 1 and * at the ends of the line prevents the most expensive misunderstanding in a system: the one in the data model."]]},
qual:{name:"Quality",about:"Quality attributes, code review, metrics and technical debt.",client:"The Ponte team",title:"The code only Caio understands",
 story:"Caio went on holiday and the bakery system went down. Bia opened the code: variables named x1 and x2, a 400-line function, not a clue about why decisions were made. It took her two days to fix one line. The code worked, but only for the person who wrote it. Ponte decided: no change reaches the main line until someone else has read it.",
 theory:[["Quality has several attributes","Beyond working, software must be readable, testable, secure, efficient and easy to change. ISO/IEC 25010 organises these attributes; maintainability weighs most on cost over the years."],
  ["Code review","Someone else reads the change before it is merged (pull request). Small, frequent reviews catch defects early, spread knowledge and keep the system from depending on a single head. The target is the code, never the author."],
  ["Technical debt and measurement","Today's shortcuts charge interest tomorrow: every change gets slower. Metrics such as cyclomatic complexity, duplication and coverage, gathered by static analysis, show where the debt piles up."]]},
devops:{name:"Continuous delivery",about:"Continuous integration and delivery, reproducible environments and rollback.",client:"Roda Viva delivery co-op",title:"\"It works on my machine\"",
 story:"The new version of the app ran perfectly on Bia's laptop. On the server it broke: a different language version, a missing library, a forgotten setting. Releasing was a manual ritual of 23 steps written in a notebook, done in the small hours once a month, always with dread. The team wants releasing to become a non-event: automatic, repeatable and reversible.",
 theory:[["Continuous integration (CI)","On every push, a server builds the project from scratch and runs all the tests. If something breaks, the team knows within minutes, while the fix is still cheap."],
  ["Continuous delivery (CD)","The same automated process carries the software all the way to production: build, tests, packaging and release. Manual steps become scripts, and what is repeatable stops being scary."],
  ["Identical environments and a quick way back","Dependencies declared in a file and standardised environments put an end to \"it works on my machine\". Small, frequent releases with a rollback at hand shrink the damage of each mistake."]]}
});
Object.assign(LANG.en.items,{
q6:{title:"Requirements triage",prompt:"Classify each requirement of the health clinic system.",bins:["Functional","Non-functional"],cards:["The patient gets a reminder the day before the appointment","The system is available 99% of the time","The receptionist reschedules appointments","Patient data is stored encrypted"],hint:"Ask each card: is this something the system DOES, or a quality with which it does it?",why:"Reminding and rescheduling are behaviours (functional). Availability and encryption are qualities of the service (non-functional), and they tend to be forgotten until they become a problem.",analogy:"On a menu, the dishes are the functional part. Arriving hot, within twenty minutes and from a clean kitchen is the non-functional part: nobody orders it, everybody expects it."},
a6:{title:"The rhythm of a sprint",prompt:"Put the Scrum sprint events in order.",lines:["Planning: the team picks what fits in the sprint","Daily meetings track progress","Review: the increment is shown to the people who use it","Retrospective: the team improves its own process"],hint:"First you decide what to do; at the end you look at the product and then at the process.",why:"The sprint opens with planning, continues with the dailies, shows the result at the review and closes with the retrospective, which feeds the next cycle.",analogy:"It is like a trip: plan the route, check the map every day, show the photos on arrival and agree on what to do differently next time."},
g6:{title:"Untangling a conflict",prompt:"Put the steps for safely resolving a merge conflict in order.",lines:["Bring the updated main into your branch","Open the files marked as conflicting","Decide, section by section, which version stays","Run the tests to check nothing broke","Record the commit that completes the merge"],hint:"The conflict only shows up when the two versions meet; the commit only comes after checking.",why:"Merging reveals the conflicts, the decision is human and made section by section, and the tests confirm the combination works before the final commit.",analogy:"Two editors changed the same paragraph: the versions are put together, read calmly, a wording is chosen, the whole text is proofread, and only then does it go to print."},
t6:{title:"The TDD cycle",prompt:"Put the test-driven development cycle in order.",lines:["Write a test that fails","Write the minimum code to make the test pass","Run all the tests and see everything green","Refactor under the protection of the tests"],hint:"Red, green, refactor.",why:"The test comes first and fails (red), the minimum code makes it pass (green), and only then is the structure improved, with the safety net of tests underneath.",analogy:"A tailor marks the measurement first, then cuts the cloth until it fits, and only at the end does the finishing, checking the measurement after every adjustment."},
d6:{title:"Signs of coupling",prompt:"Classify each situation observed in the co-op's system.",bins:["Low coupling","High coupling"],cards:["The shipping calculation receives the price table as a parameter","The screen accesses the database directly","Billing talks to payments through an interface","Changing the sign-up form forces changes in six other files"],hint:"Ask: if this part changes, how many others must change with it?",why:"Receiving dependencies from outside and talking through interfaces isolates the parts. A screen talking straight to the database and cascading changes reveal parts tied to one another.",analogy:"Appliances plugged into sockets are replaced one by one. Appliances with their wires soldered together force you to dismantle the room to change the television."},
m1:{title:"Who uses it, and what for",prompt:"Which diagram shows who uses the system and what each person can do with it?",opts:["Use case diagram","Class diagram","Sequence diagram","Deployment diagram"],hint:"Look for the view centred on people (actors) and goals.",why:"Use cases link actors to goals: the reader borrows, the volunteer registers. Classes show concepts; sequences show the order of messages.",analogy:"It is the restaurant menu seen from the dining room: who can order what, without showing the kitchen."},
m2:{title:"Book and copy",prompt:"How should the relationship between Book and Copy be modelled?",opts:["A Book has many Copies; each Copy belongs to a single Book","A Book has exactly one Copy","Many Books to many Copies","No relationship: they are the same thing"],hint:"The library has three copies of Dom Casmurro. How many titles, and how many physical objects?",why:"There is one title; the objects on the shelf are several. It is the copy that gets borrowed, not the book. Getting this multiplicity wrong contaminates the whole database.",analogy:"The recipe is one; the cakes made from it are many. You eat a cake, not the recipe."},
m3:{title:"Structure or behaviour",prompt:"Classify each sentence: which view of the model does it belong to?",bins:["Static view (classes)","Dynamic view (sequence)"],cards:["A Reader has a name and a phone number","The system checks whether a copy is available and then records the loan","A Loan refers to a Copy","A return triggers a notice to the next person on the waiting list"],hint:"Static describes what exists; dynamic describes what happens, and in what order.",why:"Attributes and relationships are structure and hold all the time. Checking, recording and notifying are events chained in time.",analogy:"The floor plan shows the rooms (static); the script of a visit shows where the person walks and in what order (dynamic)."},
m4:{title:"The loan sequence",prompt:"Put the messages of the \"borrow a book\" sequence diagram in order.",lines:["The reader asks for the book at the desk","The volunteer looks the title up in the system","The system checks whether a copy is available","The system records the loan and the due date","The volunteer hands the copy to the reader"],hint:"Each message only makes sense after the previous one has been answered.",why:"The sequence starts with the actor, goes through the lookup and the check, and only records after availability is confirmed. The physical hand-over closes the flow.",analogy:"It is a choreography: each step answers the previous one, and swapping the order makes the dancers collide."},
m5:{title:"How much to model",prompt:"The team wants to draw all 40 classes in detail before writing the first line of code. What is the best guidance?",opts:["Model what is needed to align the team and the riskiest part, and evolve the model along with the code","Model everything in detail first, so there is no rework","Model nothing: the code is the documentation","Everyone draws their own model and then it is all merged"],hint:"A model is worth the misunderstanding it prevents, not the number of boxes.",why:"Too much modelling goes stale before it is used; too little lets the misunderstanding reach the code. The right amount is enough to decide the next step safely.",analogy:"For a trip you plan the route and book the first hotel; you do not decide which table you will have lunch at on day ten."},
u1:{title:"Two days to change one line",prompt:"It took Bia two days to fix one line in Caio's code. Which quality attribute is missing?",opts:["Maintainability","Performance","Usability","Portability"],hint:"The program worked for the user. Who did it not work for?",why:"Maintainability is how easy it is to understand, fix and evolve the software. It is invisible to the user and decisive for cost.",analogy:"A car can run well and still require taking the engine apart to change a bulb. The mechanic suffers, and the owner gets the bill."},
u2:{title:"How to comment in a review",prompt:"Which code review comment is the most useful?",opts:["This loop goes through the list twice; could it be merged into one? Happy to help if you like","Awful code, redo it","Approved (without reading)","I would have done it differently"],hint:"A good comment points at a spot, explains why and opens a conversation.",why:"An effective review is specific, talks about the code rather than the person, and proposes a way forward. Approving without reading is worse than not reviewing: it creates false safety.",analogy:"A good copy editor circles the sentence and suggests another; they do not return the manuscript with \"this is bad\" written on the cover."},
u3:{title:"Paying the debt or adding to it",prompt:"Classify each action with respect to technical debt.",bins:["Pays the debt","Adds to the debt"],cards:["Renaming x1 to total_sales","Copying and pasting a function to save time","Writing tests before touching old code","Turning off the static analyser warning so the build passes"],hint:"Ask: does this make the next change easier or harder?",why:"Clear names and tests lower the cost of future changes. Duplication and silenced warnings hide problems that come back bigger.",analogy:"It is a credit card: paying in instalments solves this month, but every instalment pushed forward adds interest. Renaming and testing is paying the bill."},
u4:{title:"The 2,000-line pull request",prompt:"A change with 2,000 modified lines arrives for review. What is the best course of action?",opts:["Ask for it to be split into smaller, independent changes","Review it all in one go, in a hurry","Approve it, trusting the author","Reject it without explaining why"],hint:"A reviewer's attention drops sharply after a few hundred lines.",why:"Small changes get real attention in review, are merged sooner and are easy to revert. A giant change goes through unread.",analogy:"Nobody checks a whole house move by looking at the closed lorry. Box by box, you can see what got broken."},
u5:{title:"Where to strike first",prompt:"A function has cyclomatic complexity 35, no test coverage and is changed every week. What is the priority?",opts:["High: cover it with tests, then split it into smaller parts","Low: if it works, do not touch it","Rewrite the whole system from scratch","None: metrics mean nothing"],hint:"Combine three signals: many paths, no tests, frequent change.",why:"Risk is complexity multiplied by how often the code changes. First the safety net of tests, then refactoring in small steps; rewriting everything swaps one known risk for several unknown ones.",analogy:"It is the junction with no traffic lights that everyone crosses at rush hour: the danger is not that it exists, but how many times a day someone goes through it."},
o1:{title:"What CI does",prompt:"What does continuous integration do on every push?",opts:["Builds the project and runs the tests automatically, warning if something broke","Releases to production without testing","Only makes a backup copy of the code","Deletes old branches"],hint:"Think of a guard who checks every change as soon as it arrives.",why:"CI builds the project from scratch and runs the tests on every change. The value lies in how fast the warning comes: minutes, not weeks.",analogy:"It is the metal detector at the door: it checks each person on the way in, instead of searching the whole building at the end of the month."},
o2:{title:"The road to production",prompt:"Put the stages of a continuous delivery pipeline in order.",lines:["The developer pushes the code","The CI server fetches the code and installs the dependencies","The automated tests run","The release package is built","The version is released to production"],hint:"Nothing is packaged before passing the tests, and nothing is released before being packaged.",why:"Each stage is a gate: only what passed the tests is packaged, and only what was packaged reproducibly is released.",analogy:"An assembly line: the part arrives, is assembled, goes through quality control, is boxed, and only then leaves for the shop."},
o3:{title:"\"It works on my machine\"",prompt:"The app runs on Bia's laptop and breaks on the server. What is the most likely cause and the right fix?",opts:["Different environments; declare dependencies and versions in a file and use the same environment everywhere","A faulty server; buy another one","Bad luck; try again tomorrow","A bad language; rewrite it in another"],hint:"The code is the same in both places. What changes around it?",why:"The same code behaves differently when versions and settings change. Declared dependencies and standardised environments make execution reproducible.",analogy:"The recipe works in your kitchen and fails at the neighbour's because their oven shows a different temperature. Standardise the oven; do not blame the cake."},
o4:{title:"Release risk",prompt:"Classify each practice by its effect on release risk.",bins:["Lowers the risk","Raises the risk"],cards:["Releasing small changes several times a week","Piling up three months of changes into a single release","Having a rollback command that has been tested","Editing files directly on the production server"],hint:"Ask: if it goes wrong, will it be easy to find the cause and go back?",why:"A small change has a cause that is easy to find, and a tested rollback gives a quick way back. A giant release and manual edits in production make the error hard to locate and to undo.",analogy:"Crossing the river stone by stone lets you step back. Jumping from one bank to the other is all or nothing."},
o5:{title:"CI has been red for three days",prompt:"A test has been failing in CI for three days and the team keeps pushing code. What is the right course of action?",opts:["Stop and fix the build first: an ignored alarm stops protecting","Disable the failing test","Carry on and fix everything at the end of the month","Remove CI, which only gets in the way"],hint:"What is an alarm worth once everyone has learned to ignore it?",why:"With a red build, new defects slip in hidden behind the first one. Fixing the build becomes the whole team's priority; disabling the test merely switches off the dashboard light.",analogy:"It is the oil light on the dashboard: taping over it does not fix the engine."}
});
